{"trail": 242, "branch": "fix/retrieval-output-correctness", "base": "main", "head": "23f104600b2601809488ff812e0ac4de743061e1", "conflict": "clean", "gates": [{"gate_key": "approvals", "state": "evaluated", "outcome": "passed", "rationale": "1 approval(s) recorded", "evaluated_at_sha": "23f104600b2601809488ff812e0ac4de743061e1", "runner_ids": []}, {"gate_key": "checks", "state": "evaluated", "outcome": "passed", "rationale": "CI checks have passed", "evaluated_at_sha": "23f104600b2601809488ff812e0ac4de743061e1", "runner_ids": []}, {"gate_key": "findings", "state": "evaluated", "outcome": "passed", "rationale": "Agent review found no blocking issues", "evaluated_at_sha": "23f104600b2601809488ff812e0ac4de743061e1", "runner_ids": ["trail-review"]}, {"gate_key": "up_to_date", "state": "evaluated", "outcome": "passed", "rationale": "52 commits behind \u00b7 Mergeable", "evaluated_at_sha": "23f104600b2601809488ff812e0ac4de743061e1", "runner_ids": []}], "findings": {"counts": {"Open": 0, "OpenHigh": 0, "OpenMedium": 0, "OpenLow": 0, "Resolved": 7, "Dismissed": 0, "Stale": 5}, "findings": [], "has_more": false, "trail": {"id": "01M3ZQ6TEQC3B0SWV62KQ4RMH1", "number": 242, "branch": "fix/retrieval-output-correctness", "originalBranch": "fix/retrieval-output-correctness", "base": "main", "title": "fix(retrieval): return output an agent can accept, and disclose what was dropped", "status": "open", "phase": "reviewing", "author": {"id": "01KSK3QAE64QAADZ4Z86DY4771", "login": "suhaanthayyil"}, "assignees": ["suhaanthayyil"], "priority": "none", "type": "task", "reviewers": [{"login": "ashtom", "status": "approved"}], "createdAt": "2026-10-03T01:49:08.951Z", "updatedAt": "2026-10-05T21:34:40.272Z", "bodyDocument": {"textSnapshot": "Perfect. Now I have enough information to write the summary. Let me compile the analysis:\nProblem:\nRetrieval was failing in two ways simultaneously. MCP tools were returning results larger than clients would accept, and multiple code paths dropped work while reporting success.\nSolution:\nThe branch addresses four classes of defects:\nMCP response budget mismatch: The budget was sized to the 4 MiB transport frame instead of the agent's context window. Measured results showed brain_status at 124 KiB, brain_brief at 125 KiB, and brain_impact at 61 KiB\u2014all rejected by clients despite being under the 128 KiB budget. The budget is now 32 KiB (roughly 13k tokens), matching the smallest result observed to be refused. It is overridable via ENTIRE_BRAIN_MCP_MAX_BYTES and clamped to frame limits. Fair-share row guarantees scale with the new budget to preserve the per-list guarantee of 64 rows at 128 KiB.\nStatus verification cost: status was unusable in hooks due to per-anchor-commit git history walks. Verification now runs only behind --details, gated by the existing flag. On a 961-fact brain, status query time dropped from 24.19s to 2.42s. When details are not requested, the output now says \"verification: not run (use --details; it walks git history)\" instead of silently omitting the line.\n.brainignore filtering bug: FilterWarnings used substring matching instead of path matching, causing patterns like entire-brain (which appears in repository absolute paths) to discard every warning. The matcher now relativizes absolute warning paths against the repo root before matching. Withheld counts are disclosed in the warnings list with a new notice code, never in PartialFailures where they would mislabel failures as warnings.\nSilent truncation in export, regression, and distill paths:\nexport now reports the true total of checkpoint warnings, not the subset after filtering with --debug, and persists suppressed text to the manifest.\nregression reports the 20,000-file scan cap in the headline instead of quietly stopping and claiming \"no suspected regressions.\"\ndistill applied the 50-entry display cap before writing the manifest, so warning 51 never existed. Display and persistence caps are now separate: persistence 